      "Common Myths About Vertical Mice",
      "\"Vertical mice are less accurate than regular mice\"",
      "After the 2\u20134 week adaptation period, vertical mice with modern sensors (1600+ DPI) match regular mice for all standard productivity tasks. The MX Vertical at 4000 DPI handles multi-monitor setups and high-resolution displays without issue. You sacrifice nothing in accuracy for daily work.",
      "\"You need an expensive one for it to work\"",
      "The Anker Ergonomic Vertical Mouse at under $25 provides genuine ergonomic benefit. The core advantage \u2014 neutral forearm position \u2014 is a function of the vertical shape, not the price tag. Premium options add better sensors, wireless connectivity, and multi-device pairing, but the biomechanical benefit is present even at the budget level. See our quiet click guide (https://verticalmouseguide.com/best-quiet-click-vertical-mouse/) for office-specific recommendations.",
      "\"Vertical mice prevent carpal tunnel syndrome\"",
      "Vertical mice reduce one risk factor (pronation) associated with carpal tunnel compression. They do not prevent, treat, or cure carpal tunnel syndrome. CTS is multi-factorial \u2014 genetics, wrist anatomy, other medical conditions, and overall workstation ergonomics all play a role. If you suspect carpal tunnel, see a healthcare professional. A vertical mouse can be part of an ergonomic strategy, not a medical intervention.",
      "\"If I don't have wrist pain, I don't need one\"",
      "Repetitive strain develops gradually. By the time you feel symptoms, the underlying issue has been building for months or years. Using a vertical mouse proactively \u2014 before symptoms appear \u2014 is a reasonable preventive measure for heavy mouse users, similar to using a properly adjusted chair before you develop back pain.",
      "\"Ergonomic regular mice are just as good\"",
      "Some regular mice are marketed as \"ergonomic\" \u2014 they feature sculpted shapes, wider bodies, or thumb rests. These improvements help with comfort but do not address the fundamental problem: your forearm is still pronated. The sculpted shape makes pronation slightly more comfortable, but the forearm bones are still crossed, and the extensor muscles are still under sustained tension. A true vertical mouse changes the geometry of the problem, not just the comfort of the symptom.",
      "How to Choose Your First Vertical Mouse",
      "If you have decided to try a vertical mouse, start here:",
      "Measure your hand (https://verticalmouseguide.com/best-vertical-mouse-large-hands/#how-to-measure-your-hand) \u2014 this determines which guide to follow",
      "Set a budget \u2014 budget ($15\u201330), mid-range ($30\u201360), or premium ($60\u2013100+)",
